  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Increasing confidence is strong to severe storms late \nMonday night into early Tuesday, and then a second wave Tuesday \nafternoon and evening.\n\nA shortwave ridge crossing the area today will allow for a rain-free \nday. High temperatures will be around three to five degrees higher \nthan those realized on Sunday. Tonight, a shortwave trough will be \nprogressing southeast across the western Great Lakes region and \narrive over the Upper Ohio Valley around the early afternoon. In \nadvance of this feature, a cluster of showers and storms is expected \nto progress across KY/WV overnight, reaching the western portions of \nthe forecast area a few hours before sunrise Tuesday. This \nfirst area of convection is expected to weaken as is progresses \nsoutheast across our area, with little, if any, of it reaching \nlocations east of the crest of the Blue Ridge.\n\nNext second round of convection is expected to be associated with \ndaytime heating along and advance of the approaching upper trough \naxis. Coverage across our area should be maximized from the mid-\nafternoon through the early/late evening hours, coincident with the \nlocation of the upper trough. One limiting factor could be \nresidual debris cloud cover from the morning convection limiting\nnet solar radiation across parts of the region.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center is still highlighting the potential for \nstrong to severe storms with both rounds of convection, with the \nafternoon/evening bout having the greatest potential. For either \nround of convection, damaging thunderstorm winds are the greatest \nthreat for severe weather.\n\nKey Message 2: Near normal temperatures and no notable precipitation \nchances after Tuesday until the weekend.\n\nA building upper ridge over the Mississippi Valley, and surface \nhigh pressure building into our region, will keep precipitation \nto the west and east of the area Wednesday through Friday. A \nshortwave trough is expected to head across the Great Lakes \nregion this weekend. With it will come increasing southerly and \nmoist flow into our region with showers and storms again \npossible on Saturday and Sunday. The system's upper trough axis \nis expected to cross our region Sunday afternoon or evening. \nTemperatures will be near normal for this time of year.",
